<Legal
  title="Cookie Notice"
  description="PetPomo sets no cookies. Here is exactly what it stores in your browser instead, and why."
  updated="2026-08-16"
  intro="PetPomo sets no cookies at all. This page explains what it does use, so you can check for yourself."
>
  <h2>No cookies</h2>
  <p>
    There is no cookie banner here because there is nothing to consent to. PetPomo sets no cookies — not for
    analytics, not for advertising, not for sessions. You can confirm this in your browser's developer tools under
    <em>Application → Cookies</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does use: local storage</h2>
  <p>
    Local storage holds your game so it is still there when you come back. Unlike cookies it is never transmitted to
    a server automatically — it stays in your browser until you clear it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>petpomo.save.v1</code> — coins, unlocks, pet mood, settings and session history.</li>
    <li><code>petpomo.timer.v1</code> — the current timer, stored as an absolute end time so it survives a refresh.</li>
    <li><code>petpomo.lastSeen.v1</code> — when you were last here, to work out how hungry the pet got.</li>
    <li><code>petpomo.weather.v1</code> — the last weather reading, cached for 30 minutes to avoid re-asking.</li>
    <li><code>petpomo.weather.absent.v1</code> — set only if the weather endpoint is unavailable, so we stop asking.</li>
    <li><code>petpomo.syncCode.v1</code> — your sync code, but only if you turned cloud sync on.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Clearing it</h2>
  <p>
    Clearing site data for this domain removes all of it and resets the game. There is a reset button in
    <a href="/settings">Settings</a> too, along with an export so you can keep a copy first.
  </p>

  <h2>Third parties</h2>
  <p>
    No third-party scripts run on this site — no analytics, no ad network, no embedded widgets, no external fonts.
    The requests the site makes on your behalf all go to its own endpoints: <code>/api/weather</code> for the sky,
    <code>/api/sync</code> if you turn cloud sync on, and <code>/api/ask</code> when you talk to the pet. All three are
    described in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is one exception worth stating plainly, and it is not a cookie. If you press the microphone button to talk to
    your pet <strong>in Chrome or Edge</strong>, the browser itself sends the audio to Google's speech service to turn
    it into text. That is the browser doing it rather than a script of ours, but the effect on you is the same, so it
    is written down here as well as in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>. It happens only while you hold that
    feature open, and never otherwise.
  </p>
  <p>
    If advertising is added later it will very likely involve cookies. This page and the privacy policy will be
    updated first, and a consent banner will be shown where the law requires one.
  </p>
</Legal>
